## Project Deliverable and Structure

### What I am building
A short, readable UX case study published on GitHub.  
It compares London and Shanghai by looking at how a first-time visitor experiences the city.

This is not a technical report or a data-heavy project.  
It is a product-style case study focused on user experience and systems.

---

### Who this project is for
- A first-time visitor or newcomer
- Someone unfamiliar with the city and its systems
- Someone relying on public transport, digital tools and signage

---

### What I am analysing
I am analysing everyday journeys through three city systems:

1. Transport  
   How people move around the city using public transport.

2. Payments  
   How people pay for transport, food and everyday services.

3. Wayfinding and guidance  
   How the city communicates information to users through signs, apps, maps and instructions.

---

### What I am NOT doing
- I am not evaluating policy or government performance.
- I am not ranking which city is better.
- I am not building a data-heavy dashboard.
- I am not doing academic research.

---

### Format of the work
The project will be written and visual, not technical.

It will include:
- Short written sections
- Simple comparison tables
- A few basic diagrams or sketches

---

### Structure of the case study

The project will be organised into five short sections:

1. Overview  
   What the project is and why it exists.

2. Method and scope  
   Who the user is, what I am analysing, and what I am not analysing.

3. Journey comparisons  
   Side-by-side comparison of London and Shanghai for:
   - transport
   - payments
   - wayfinding and guidance

4. Friction and insights  
   Where users experience confusion, friction or uncertainty.

5. Design opportunities  
   A small number of realistic ideas to improve the user experience.

---

### Length and level of detail
- Total length: around 1,500 to 2,000 words
- Easy to read in one sitting
- Clear and structured, not long or academic

---

### Goal of the project
To show how product and UX thinking can be applied to real-world systems like cities, using clear reasoning, good structure and thoughtful insights.
